# Catalog songs

Editorial recordings live in `src/lib/catalog/songs.ts` (not the MIDI
library). House style for lyrics and sourcing:
`.cursor/rules/history-articles.mdc`.

**Type:** the song page is a long-form entry. Lyrics, captions, credits,
links, and pills are **16px minimum** (`text-base`). Never `text-sm`,
`text-xs`, or a custom size under `1rem`. `about` paragraphs follow the
reading-column writing rules: 2–4 sentences, split with `\n\n`. See “Type
size” and “Reading column” in `history-articles.mdc`.

## Required on every new / expanded song

- Verified `youtubeId`, or `listen` to an archive when there is no legal
  player. Never invent an id. Several versions go in `recordings`, lead
  version first, and the page plays them as YouTube chips. See “Players”
  below.
- `genres` when an **existing** genre slug fits. Omit rather than invent.
- **Sourced `lyrics` on the song page.** Hunt first; copyright is not a
  skip. See “Lyrics — always hunt” below.
- **Non-English songs carry `original`** — the English name *and* the
  home-language name, both searchable. See “Bilingual titles” below.
- **The person is not a byline.** When the entry is about someone, include
  a few sourced facts from the life that make the work make sense. See
  “People are not bylines” in `.cursor/rules/history-articles.mdc`.
- **A name with no artist page links to Wikipedia** when an article
  exists and we are not adding an entry. See
  `.cursor/rules/person-names.mdc`. Avraham Reisen stays an `<ArtistLink>`;
  his sister Sarah, his brother Zalman, and Peretz on the sukkah page
  are the Wikipedia case. Kalman Reisen has no article, so his name
  stays plain.
- **`places` — the map.** A live song whose prose names a city must pin
  those ids. See “Map” below. Genre slugs alone do not draw a map.

## Map — pin the places, or the page stays blank

The song page resolves a map in this order (`songPageMapEntity`):

1. `song.places`, in the order given.
2. Else places derived from `genres` / `history`.
3. Else the **credited** artists’ `places` (not `mentioned`).

Step 2 only works for genres the place registry actually tags:
**klezmer, blues, rock, yiddish-theater**. `yiddish-folk`, `chasidic`,
and `traditional-jewish` match nothing. That is why “A sukkele a kleyne”
shipped with no map: the about names Kraków, Warsaw, Berlin, Chernivtsi,
Worms, and New York, and the page never received a `places` list.

- List **every place the prose cites** — where it was written, printed,
  arranged, learned, recorded. Only what the text supports.
- If the id is missing from `src/lib/places/registry.ts`, **add a stub**
  in the same change (a point and a blurb from a fact already on the page).
  Do **not** put a broad `genres` tag on that stub. A tag drops the city
  onto every page that derives its map from that genre.
- Do not skip `places` and rely on the artist fallback. The fallback is
  there so an old row is not blank. A new or expanded song sets its own.
- A live **artist** whose bio names places needs `artist.places` the same
  way. Their page does not inherit the song’s pins.
- **Israel is always on the map for Jewish repertoire.** A traditional
  Jewish, Chasidic, Yiddish, klezmer, or Yiddish-theater song is sung in
  Israel today — that is where much of the living audience is. Pin
  `"israel"` on every such song, and say so once in the prose (who
  sings it there, or a Hebrew version, when a source names one). The
  2026-09 sukkah entry shipped with Kraków, Berlin, and New York and
  no Israel; do not repeat that. Same for a genre or cousins article
  in that repertoire.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — about names Kraków and New York; yiddish-folk derives nothing
genres: ["yiddish-folk"]

// ❌ BAD — a Yiddish song mapped only to where it was written and printed
places: ["krakow", "warsaw", "berlin", "new-york"]

// ✅ GOOD — the journey, and where it is still sung
places: ["krakow", "warsaw", "berlin", "new-york", "israel"]
```

## Lyrics — always hunt

Hearing a recording without the words is half a lesson. A new or
expanded song **must** get a sourced `lyrics` block, or a written reason
in `about` after a real hunt. Do **not** leave lyrics off because the
composition is still under copyright — that is how Over the Rainbow
shipped empty. Still-protected words are printed for teaching, with a
citable source, a link out, and the fair-use note `<SongLyrics>` already
shows.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — copyright used as a skip
about: "We do not print the English lyric here: it is still under copyright."

// ✅ GOOD — sourced, attributed, fair-use footer already on the page
lyrics: [{
  id: "english",
  label: "English — Harburg",
  lang: "en",
  language: "English",
  source: { label: "Leo Feist, Inc., 1939", url: "https://…" },
  lines: [/* transcribed from that publication, never from memory */],
}]
```

**Hunt order:** scholarly collections (Mlotek / Workers Circle for
Yiddish), **Yiddish Song of the Week**
([yiddishsong.wordpress.com](https://yiddishsong.wordpress.com/))
for any Yiddish song, Wikisource / IMSLP / LOC / university sheet-music
scans for public-domain songs, a cited encyclopedia or Wikipedia table
that actually prints the words, published sheet music we can point at.
Web-search; **never invent a line or “fix” one you did not see.**

When the singing and the printing differ, and the site’s developer supplies
the sung lines, say “the developer” in `credit` and link that phrase to
`/about`. Do not use a personal name. Leave `source` as the publication
that was checked. Do not credit the developer as the author of the song.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — role, linked to the about page; the source stays the printing
credit: "The developer supplied these lines. They are the wording large crowds sing, where the printed sources differ.",
creditLink: { phrase: "The developer", href: "/about" },
source: { label: "Anash.org, 17 October 2019", url: "https://…" },
```

**Yiddish songs — check this site before you stop.** Itzik Gottesman’s
[Yiddish Song of the Week](https://yiddishsong.wordpress.com/) posts
field recordings with notes, who sang it, where they learned it, and
often a transcription plus an English translation. Search the blog for
the title and for the singer. Read the comments: a reader sometimes
supplies the verse the post left out (the Ha-rachaman line on
[“A sikele, a kleyne”](https://yiddishsong.wordpress.com/2010/10/05/a-sikele-a-kleyne-performed-by-beyle-schaechter-gottesman/)
is that kind of find). A Yiddish lyric or history hunt that never
opened this site is not finished.

**The only honest omissions** (say so in `about`):

1. **Instrumental** — no words exist (Dale’s Misirlou, Brandwein
   bulgars). Do not invent a lyric.
2. **Author forbade reprint** — e.g. Al Grand asked that his Yiddish
   Over the Rainbow not be used without permission. Hunt anyway; if the
   ask is explicit, omit and quote the request.
3. **Hunt failed** — no citable published text (not “I remembered it”).
   Write what you searched. A later pass must try again.

Non-English versions: original + line-by-line English gloss (`en`),
romanization (`latin`) when the script isn’t Latin. Several sung
versions → several `SongLyrics` entries. Don’t paste a lyric site with
no provenance (Genius, AZLyrics). Wikipedia / Wikisource / Milken /
Mlotek / LOC / Yiddish Song of the Week are fine when they print the words.

## Lyrics and recordings — one section, one card per version

Every song page renders its recordings and its words through
**`<SongLyrics>`** (`src/components/catalog/SongLyrics.tsx`), and
nowhere else on the page:

```tsx
<SongLyrics
  title={song.title}
  versions={song.lyrics ?? []}
  recordings={songPageRecordings(song)}
/>
```

Do not hand-roll a second layout, an always-open stack, or a client
toggle that leaves the words out of the HTML. Do not render the
recordings again above the section. **A version is one card**: the
player, then the lyrics it sings. That is how the reader and the
crawler find both together.

- Near the top of the page, before the history, an **On this page**
  label. First entry is **Song history**, linking to `#history` on the
  article. Next is the jump to `#lyrics`. Those link words match the
  section: **Lyrics, translation, and recordings** when all three are
  there; drop a word the page does not have; **Recordings** when there
  are no words. A speaker icon (`Volume2`) sits at the end of the
  lyrics link, every time. A rule and space separate that list from the
  history, so the article has a start. Both targets carry `scroll-mt-24`.
  Do not leave the jumps as bare links, and do not bury the only way
  to the words under the essay.

- Pair them in data. A recording names the version it sings with
  `lyrics: "<version id>"`. The section builds cards in `lyrics` order:
  a version pulls in every recording that names it, and the words
  print under each (two singers of the same text → two cards, same
  lines — duplicating the words on the page is fine). A version no
  recording sings is a words-only card. A recording that names no
  version comes last, as an audio-only card. Set `lyrics` only when
  that performance sings those lines; never guess. A song with **one**
  recording and **one** version pairs them on its own
  (`songPageRecordings`); with more of either, name the pairs.
- YouTube is a `CollapsibleVideo` chip; a file we host is
  `HostedAudioPlayer`. Both live inside the card. The **closed** card
  already shows what it holds: a YouTube mark (red) or a speaker
  (`Volume2`) beside the chevron. Words-only cards show neither.
- The heading names what the page actually has: **Lyrics, translation,
  and recordings of “{title}”** when all three are there; drop
  “translation” or “recordings” when they are not; **Recordings of
  “{title}”** when there are no words at all. One sentence under it
  says **Song lyrics for “{title}”** (and “with an English translation
  under each line” when a gloss exists), then that each version is one
  entry — the recording and the words it sings. Do not pile extra
  synonyms into the prose. Further queries (`lyrics of`, `recording`,
  `audio`, `history of`) live in `songQueryPhrases` and the page
  title, description, and FAQ.
- Each card is a `<details>`. **More than one card: all closed**, so
  the reader chooses. A single card starts open. The lines stay in the
  static HTML either way. An in-page link (`#lyrics-<id>`, the
  `seeAlso` field) names the first card of that version; the browser
  opens it.

## Bilingual titles (non-English songs)

A song whose title is not English **must** name the tune in both languages,
and the global search bar (⌘K) **must** find it by either. English speakers
type “over the rainbow”; a Yiddish speaker types “iber dem regnboygn” or
`איבער דעם רעגן־בויגן`. Both land on the same song.

`title` stays the **best-known Latin display form** (unchanged for existing
rows — `Iber dem regnboygn`, `Bei Mir Bistu Shein`, `Misirlou`). The other
forms live in a structured `original` field:

```ts
/** Present iff the song’s title is not English. */
original?: {
  /** English title, or a literal gloss when there is no English title
   *  ("Over the Rainbow"; or "‘The calf’" for Dos kelbl). Required. */
  english: string;
  /** BCP-47 of the original language ("yi", "el", "ro"). Required. */
  lang: string;
  /** Romanization when the script isn’t Latin (YIVO for Yiddish, etc.).
   *  Usually equal to `title`; set it when `title` is a stylized variant. */
  latin?: string;
  /** Native-script spelling — **only when attested**. Never invent letters
   *  (YIVO for Yiddish, unpointed Hebrew, monotonic Greek). Omit if unsure. */
  native?: string;
};
```

Honesty (same bar as loanwords, `.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc`):
`native` is an attested spelling or nothing — cite it in a short comment,
never guess script. `english` is the real English title when one exists; a
plain-language translation in quotes otherwise. Don’t label a proper-noun
tune (Misirlou) with a fake “English title” — give its native script + a
glossed meaning.

**Display** (render, don’t hand-format per page): `songTitleParts(song)` in
`songs.ts` returns `{ display, english?, native?, lang? }`. Surfaces:

- **Song page header, `/songs` cards, search rows** — show both: `title`
  with `— “{english}”`, and the native script (via `<NativeScript>`,
  dir-aware) when present. Never put `dir="rtl"` on a box that also holds
  Latin — see the loanword RTL note.
- **`<SongLink>` in article prose** keeps the single `title` inline (prose
  stays readable); the panel it opens shows the full bilingual title.

**Search** (both surfaces, same change):

- Global (`src/lib/search/index.ts`) — fold `original.english`,
  `original.latin`, `original.native`, and the language name into the song
  entry, and treat `english` as a **title alias** so an English-title query
  ranks it like a title hit, not a body hit.
- Hub (`src/lib/catalog/search.ts` → `songHaystack`) — add the same fields.

English-titled songs (blues catalog, `Over the Rainbow`) leave `original`
unset — they’re already in one language.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — Yiddish song, both names, attested native script
{
  slug: "iber-dem-regnboygn",
  title: "Iber dem regnboygn",
  original: {
    english: "Over the Rainbow",
    lang: "yi",
    native: "איבער דעם רעגן־בויגן", // Grand’s Forward spelling, 2003
  },
  // …
}
```

## Players — the YouTube chip

A song page does not mount an open YouTube iframe. Every playable
recording is a **chip**: `CollapsibleVideo`
(`src/components/media/CollapsibleVideo.tsx`). The row shows the YouTube
mark and the recording label; a click expands `YouTubeEmbed` in place
(one song at a time, via `youtubeConductor`). The same chip is what
cousins articles use. `<SongLink>` uses the same row inside its panel.
Do not add a second player, a raw iframe, or a heading-plus-embed stack
on `src/app/songs/[slug]/page.tsx`.

A recording **we host** (an mp3 under `public/audio/`) uses
`HostedAudioPlayer` (`src/components/media/HostedAudioPlayer.tsx`) — the
same one-song rule, our own play control, nothing loaded until the
click. Do not drop a raw `<audio controls>` on a song page. A one-shot
word clip stays `PronounceButton`. Credit the page the file came from
in the version’s `source` and `note`. Put the file in `recordings`
with `src` (no `youtubeId`) so it gets a card like the YouTube chips.

`recordings` is the order `<SongLink>`, cousins lists, and the sources
footer use, and the order of audio-only cards. Cards with words follow
`lyrics` order. **The version this entry leads with is first.**
`youtubeId` on the song matches the first YouTube entry in that list.
Later versions follow,
each with a `label` that names who is singing and the year when we know
it. A single upload needs no `recordings` array — the page builds one
chip from `youtubeId`.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — open players, or the lead version buried last
youtubeId: "O2fYLhrejvQ",
recordings: [
  { youtubeId: "O2fYLhrejvQ", label: "Fishl Beigel, 1991" },
  { youtubeId: "BGt3ASrzFNg", label: "The Rabbis’ Sons, 1968" },
]

// ✅ GOOD — lead recording first; youtubeId matches it; each names the
// lyric version it sings, so its card carries those words
youtubeId: "BGt3ASrzFNg",
recordings: [
  { youtubeId: "BGt3ASrzFNg", label: "The Rabbis’ Sons, 1968 — the Chasidic rewording" },
  { youtubeId: "O2fYLhrejvQ", label: "Fishl Beigel with Suki & Ding, 1991", lyrics: "chasidic-chorus" },
]
```

## If a Cousins article names this song

Both directions, same change:

1. The article `members` list includes this slug.
2. This song has `cousins: ["<article-slug>"]`.

The song page **must** show those cousin links at the **top** (title →
attribution → genre pills → `COUSIN_SONG_HEADING` / `cousinSongLinkLabel`
from `src/lib/cousins/registry.ts`). Copy standard:
`.cursor/rules/cousins.mdc`. Don’t bury the card under the players.
`getCousinsBySong` is the lookup — don’t hand-roll a second list.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — mentioned in /cousins/miserlou, no reverse, no pills on either side
slug: "misirlou-dale"

// ✅ GOOD
slug: "misirlou-dale",
cousins: ["miserlou"],
genres: ["rock"],
```

Name a person → catalog artist + `<ArtistLink>` on **every** mention,
not only the first. A surname used alone goes on `alsoNamed`.
Name a foreign word → `WORDS` + first-mention wrap (human clip or no
speaker). `.cursor/rules/loanword-speakers.mdc`.
Keep one slug per recording/composition; don’t fork a second copy.

## Sources — the bottom of the page

Every song, artist, and genre spoke ends with **Sources & further reading**.
It is the last block on the page, after the map. The component is
`EntrySources` (`src/components/content/EntrySources.tsx`). It uses the
same numbered list as a history article (`SourceList`). Do not add a
second design, and do not park the credits in the header.

- **Wikipedia, when a page exists.** A song or artist with a Wikipedia
  article links it here. Check English and the language of the work
  (Yiddish, Hebrew, and so on). Label English as “Wikipedia — {name}”
  and any other edition by its language (“Hebrew Wikipedia — the
  song”). Verify the URL. No article means no link — do not point at
  a search page, a different topic, or every language edition that
  merely mentions the name.
- **Song.** `links` is the bibliography: printings, essays, archives, in
  the order a reader should open them. The page also appends each lyric
  version’s `source`, an archive `listen` link, and each recording
  (`songSources()`). Same URL once. The sukkah page is the pattern —
  Gottesman’s article, then the 1902 printing and the later scores, not
  a mid-page “Early printings” list.
- **Artist.** `links` (encyclopedias, archives, their own book). Verify
  the URL. Do not fabricate one to fill the slot.
- **Genre.** `links` on the genre, same footer. A history article’s own
  `SourceList` does not replace this; the genre page’s claims need their
  own credits.
- An empty list still shows the heading and “Sources for this page are
  not listed yet.” That line means the entry is unfinished. A `live`
  page that states facts does not ship with it.
- Credit the page a file or lyric came from. A recording we host still
  names that article here, not only beside the player.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — credits tucked under the intro, or a live page with no sources
<section>Early printings</section>

// ✅ GOOD — last on the page, numbered, the title is the link
links: [
  { label: "Itzik Gottesman, Yiddish Song of the Week, 5 October 2010", url: "https://…" },
  { label: "Hebrew Wikipedia — the song", url: "https://he.wikipedia.org/…" },
  { label: "Reisen, Ale verk, vol. 5 (New York, 1917) — HathiTrust", url: "https://…" },
]
```
